EXTRA = [
    {"after": "diet", "id": "hd-nutrition", "nav": "Eating on HD", "tag": "Clinical application",
     "h2": "How eating changes once hemodialysis starts",
     "html": """
<p>Many patients reach hemodialysis (HD) after months or years of being told to eat less: less protein, less salt, fewer fruits. Once dialysis starts, some of that advice reverses and some becomes stricter. Nobody tells them which is which unless you do. Patients who are not told clearly often keep eating the low-protein diet they learned before dialysis, and they lose weight and muscle. The table earlier in this module set out the targets by stage; this section explains what changes on HD and why.</p>
<p><strong>Protein goes up.</strong> Each session removes amino acids into the dialysate, and the treatment itself and the chronic inflammation of kidney failure increase protein breakdown. For a patient on HD who is metabolically stable, the KDOQI 2020 nutrition guideline recommends a protein intake of 1.0 to 1.2 g/kg of body weight per day, with energy of 25 to 35 kcal/kg per day adjusted for age, sex, activity, body composition, and inflammation.[[nutr2020]] In practice this usually means a protein food at every meal. The patient&rsquo;s individual target is set by the physician and dietitian; the nurse&rsquo;s job is to make sure the patient knows that the pre-dialysis limit no longer applies.</p>
<p><strong>Sodium and fluid stay tight.</strong> KDOQI recommends limiting sodium to less than 100 mmol (2.3 g) a day in people on dialysis, as in earlier stages, to help control blood pressure and fluid.[[nutr2020]] On HD the reason becomes visible: almost all the weight a patient gains between sessions is salt and the water drunk to satisfy the thirst it causes. A large gain means a high hourly removal rate at the next session, with cramps, low blood pressure, and the longer-term harm described in Module 6. Fluid allowances are individual and depend on remaining urine output. When the gain is large, the first question is about salty food, not about how much water the patient drank.</p>
<p><strong>Potassium depends on the day.</strong> Potassium accumulates between sessions and is removed during them, so the risk is highest just before treatment, and highest of all before the first session after the long gap between sessions (for most patients, the weekend). KDOQI suggests adjusting dietary potassium to keep the blood level in the normal range rather than applying a fixed limit.[[nutr2020]] The practical teaching is to keep high-potassium foods small on the days without dialysis, particularly the day before the long-gap session, and to report a high pre-dialysis potassium together with a diet recall so that the dietitian and physician can see the cause. Constipation and some medicines also raise potassium, as described earlier in this module, and are checked before diet is blamed.</p>
<p><strong>Phosphate and binders.</strong> Dialysis removes phosphate poorly compared with the amount eaten, so diet and binders remain necessary. A higher protein intake also brings more phosphate, so the dietitian steers patients toward protein foods with relatively less phosphate and away from processed foods with phosphate additives. Binders work only when they meet food in the gut: they are taken with meals and with snacks that contain phosphate, as the prescription for the specific product states. A patient who eats a heavy merienda without a binder, or who takes the binder with only coffee, gets little benefit.</p>
<p><strong>Water-soluble vitamins.</strong> B vitamins and vitamin C dissolve in water and are lost into the dialysate, and restricted diets may already be low in them. KDOQI suggests folate, vitamin B12, or B-complex supplements to correct deficiency, and considers vitamin C supplementation for patients at risk of deficiency to reach the recommended intake of at least 90 mg a day for men and 75 mg a day for women.[[nutr2020]] Supplements are taken as prescribed, usually after the session on dialysis days. Over-the-counter multivitamins or high-dose vitamin C, sold freely in pharmacies and by relatives, may contain amounts or ingredients unsuited to kidney failure; the patient is asked to show any supplement to the physician or pharmacist before taking it.</p>
<p><strong>Screening for malnutrition.</strong> Protein-energy wasting is common on HD and is associated with worse outcomes. KDOQI considers routine nutrition screening at least twice a year reasonable, finds limited evidence for choosing one screening tool over another, recommends the 7-point Subjective Global Assessment as a valid tool for assessing nutritional status in patients on dialysis, and suggests the Malnutrition-Inflammation Score may also be used.[[nutr2020]] These assessments are performed by the dietitian or a trained clinician under unit policy. The nurse supplies the evidence that triggers them: a falling post-dialysis weight, loose clothes, poor appetite, a meal skipped on dialysis days, a falling albumin, and the warning signs listed earlier in this module.</p>
<p><strong>Eating during treatment.</strong> Practice varies between units. Some allow a light snack or an oral nutrition supplement during the session, which can help patients who leave home early and return late; KDOQI suggests a trial of at least 3 months of oral nutrition supplements when counseling alone does not meet the needs of a patient at risk of wasting.[[nutr2020]] Others discourage eating on the machine, because digestion draws blood to the gut and can lower blood pressure, and because eating while lying back carries a risk of choking if the patient becomes drowsy or nauseated (Module 9). Follow the unit policy. A patient prone to low blood pressure is better advised to eat before arriving or after the session, and a patient with diabetes needs an individual plan agreed with the physician, because skipping a meal after taking insulin or tablets can cause low blood sugar during treatment.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>What patients often believe</th><th>What to teach on HD</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>&ldquo;I must keep protein low to protect my kidneys.&rdquo;</td><td>On HD, protein needs rise; aim for a protein food at each meal, as the dietitian advises</td></tr>
<tr><td>&ldquo;Dialysis removes everything, so I can eat anything on dialysis day.&rdquo;</td><td>Dialysis removes only part of what accumulates; salt, potassium, and phosphate still count every day</td></tr>
<tr><td>&ldquo;If I drink less water, I can eat salty food.&rdquo;</td><td>Salt causes thirst; the gain comes back as fluid. Cutting salt is what makes the fluid limit bearable</td></tr>
<tr><td>&ldquo;Vitamins are always good for you.&rdquo;</td><td>Only the vitamins prescribed; some over-the-counter products are unsafe in kidney failure</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>On HD, protein goes up and salt stays down. Say both, in those words, at the first session and again whenever the weight falls.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "hd-nutrition", "id": "hd-meals", "nav": "Filipino meals on HD", "tag": "Clinical application",
     "h2": "Filipino meals on dialysis days",
     "html": """
<p>Advice that ignores what is on the family table is forgotten by the next meal. The most useful counseling starts from what the patient actually ate yesterday and changes the portion, the preparation, or one ingredient at a time. The renal dietitian sets the individual plan; the nurse, who sees the patient every session, reinforces it in familiar food terms and notices when it is not working.</p>
<p><strong>Building the plate.</strong> A simple pattern for many patients on HD is a measured portion of rice, a palm-sized portion of protein food at each meal, and a serving of a lower-potassium vegetable, cooked with less salt. Rice and other plain starches provide energy without much potassium or phosphate; a patient who is losing weight should not be told to cut rice without the dietitian&rsquo;s advice.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Usual choice</th><th>Practical swap or adjustment</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Tuyo, daing, or other dried and salted fish</td><td>Fresh fish such as galunggong or tilapia, fried or grilled with little salt</td><td>Same protein, much less sodium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hotdog, longganisa, tocino, corned beef, luncheon meat</td><td>Eggs, fresh chicken, or fresh fish, cooked at home</td><td>Processed meats carry both salt and phosphate additives</td></tr>
<tr><td>Instant noodles with the full seasoning packet</td><td>Plain noodles or rice with home-cooked fish or egg; if instant noodles are eaten, use little or none of the packet</td><td>Most of the sodium is in the seasoning</td></tr>
<tr><td>Generous fish sauce, soy sauce, or bagoong at the table</td><td>Calamansi, vinegar, garlic, onion, ginger, or pepper for flavor; keep the bottle off the table</td><td>Flavor without sodium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Banana or ripe mango as the daily fruit</td><td>Apple, pineapple, pear, or grapes in an ordinary portion</td><td>Lower potassium, as listed earlier in this module</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sweet potato, taro, or mung beans</td><td>Small portions, peeled, cut small, and boiled in plenty of water that is discarded, if the dietitian permits</td><td>Preparation lowers potassium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Soft drinks, especially cola, and chocolate drinks</td><td>Water within the fluid allowance, or a small portion of a lower-potassium fruit</td><td>Cola carries phosphate; chocolate drinks carry potassium and phosphate; both count as fluid</td></tr>
<tr><td>A &ldquo;low-salt&rdquo; salt substitute</td><td>Do not use unless the physician approves</td><td>Many are potassium chloride and can raise potassium dangerously</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Sabaw and soup.</strong> Sinigang, tinola, nilaga, bulalo, and other soups are central to Filipino meals and are often the hardest change. The broth is a problem in two ways: it is fluid that counts toward the day&rsquo;s allowance, and it collects salt, seasoning cubes or sour mixes, and potassium that has leached out of the vegetables and meat. Practical advice: eat the solid pieces and take only a small cup of broth, counted as fluid; cook with fresh tamarind, calamansi, or other sour fruit and less salt rather than a seasoning mix, if the family is willing; choose lower-potassium vegetables such as sayote, upo, or cabbage for the pot; and avoid drinking soup to &ldquo;wash down&rdquo; rice. Rice porridge also counts as fluid.</p>
<p><strong>Fiestas, birthdays, and family gatherings.</strong> Celebrations are where many large weight gains begin: lechon with its sauce, pancit seasoned with soy sauce, kare-kare with bagoong, dinuguan, cakes, and soft drinks, often over a long weekend before the long-gap session. Forbidding the patient to attend does not work and harms quality of life. A plan agreed in advance works better:</p>
<ul>
<li>Eat a small home meal before going, so that hunger does not choose the plate.</li>
<li>Use a small plate, take one portion, and choose grilled or roasted dishes over saucy ones; go easy on sauces and bagoong.</li>
<li>Bring or take the binder and use it with the meal.</li>
<li>Count every drink, including soft drinks and soup; ice chips or a small cup help.</li>
<li>Be most careful when the gathering falls before the long gap between sessions.</li>
<li>Tell the unit afterward. Honest reporting allows the next session to be planned safely, and it is not a confession to be scolded.</li>
</ul>
<p>Families often bring food to show care. Inviting the person who cooks to a counseling session, or giving them the swap list, often changes more than talking to the patient alone.</p>
<p><strong>Protein on a tight budget.</strong> Higher protein needs come at a time when dialysis has strained the household&rsquo;s finances. Eggs are among the least expensive sources of good-quality protein; egg whites provide protein with less phosphate than the yolk, and the dietitian advises how many whole eggs suit the patient. Small, inexpensive fresh fish, chicken parts, and tofu in moderate portions are other options. Processed meats and canned goods are cheap and convenient, but their sodium and phosphate additives make them poor value for a patient on HD. If cost is the reason a patient eats too little protein, refer to the social worker as well as the dietitian; that is a financial problem, not a knowledge problem.</p>
<p><strong>Teach-back.</strong> Teach-back means asking the patient to explain or show, in their own words, what they will do, then correcting and repeating until the explanation is right; a systematic review linked it to better understanding and recall across many settings, although the included studies varied in design and quality.[[talevski2020]] Questions that work in food counseling:</p>
<ul>
<li>&ldquo;Tell me what you will have for lunch tomorrow, which is a non-dialysis day.&rdquo;</li>
<li>&ldquo;Show me how much sabaw you will take with your sinigang.&rdquo;</li>
<li>&ldquo;When will you take your binder at your granddaughter&rsquo;s birthday party?&rdquo;</li>
<li>&ldquo;What will you use instead of fish sauce?&rdquo;</li>
</ul>
<p>Record what was taught, who was present, and how understanding was checked. Then use the next session&rsquo;s weight gain, potassium, and phosphate as feedback, and celebrate improvements as clearly as you report problems.</p>
<p><strong>Start from yesterday&rsquo;s plate, change one thing at a time, and check it with teach-back.</strong></p>
"""},
]
